@node Binary Fixed-Point (BINARY)
@section Binary Fixed-Point (BINARY)

@subsection Characteristics

BINARY types represent unsigned binary integers with no decimal point. They are stored as
raw binary values in registers or memory with 8, 16, or 32-bit width typical in EIGHTBOL targets.

@table @asis
@item Size
Typically 1, 2, or 4 bytes (8, 16, 32 bits)

@item Range
For N bits: 0 to 2^N - 1

@item Signedness
Unsigned (all bits represent magnitude)

@item Overflow Behavior
Wraps silently; overflow to 0, 1, 2, ... (modulo 2^N)

@item Example: 8-bit BINARY
@code{PIC 9(3) -> BINARY 8-bit -> range 0-255}
Storing 300 results in 300 MOD 256 = 44

@item Example: 16-bit BINARY
@code{PIC 9(5) -> BINARY 16-bit -> range 0-65535}
@end table

@subsection Arithmetic with BINARY

When performing arithmetic with BINARY operands:

@itemize
@item Addition: Result wraps at 2^N boundary
@item Subtraction: If result is negative, wraps (e.g., 5 - 10 = -5 becomes 256 - 5 = 251 in 8-bit)
@item Multiplication: High bits are truncated
@item Division: Integer division; remainder is discarded
@end itemize

@subsection Cross-Language Notes

@itemize
@item COBOL: @code{PIC 9(n)} declarations with USAGE BINARY
@item FORTRAN: @code{INTEGER} without explicit sign
@item Forth: Native machine integers (cell size dependent)
@item Objective-C: @code{unsigned int}, @code{unsigned long}
@item Pascal: @code{word}, @code{longword}, @code{cardinal}
@item Lua: Integers in default implementation
@end itemize

@node Binary Signed (BINARY SIGNED)
@section Binary Signed (BINARY SIGNED)

@subsection Characteristics

BINARY SIGNED types represent signed binary integers using two's complement representation.
They provide a full range of negative and positive values within an N-bit word.

@table @asis
@item Size
Typically 1, 2, or 4 bytes (8, 16, 32 bits)

@item Range
For N bits: -(2^(N-1)) to 2^(N-1) - 1

@item Signedness
Signed (one bit for sign, N-1 bits for magnitude)

@item Overflow Behavior
Wraps silently; overflow to negative values, underflow to positive values

@item Example: 8-bit BINARY SIGNED
@code{PIC S9(2) -> BINARY SIGNED 8-bit -> range -128 to 127}
Storing 200 results in 200 - 256 = -56

@item Example: 16-bit BINARY SIGNED
@code{PIC S9(4) -> BINARY SIGNED 16-bit -> range -32768 to 32767}
@end table

@subsection Arithmetic with BINARY SIGNED

When performing arithmetic with BINARY SIGNED operands:

@itemize
@item Addition: Honors signs; overflow wraps (e.g., 127 + 1 = -128 in 8-bit)
@item Subtraction: Honors signs; underflow wraps
@item Multiplication: Result sign is determined by XOR of operand signs
@item Division: Result sign is determined by XOR of operand signs (round toward zero)
@end itemize

@subsection Two's Complement Detail

Two's complement representation allows symmetric range around zero. The most significant bit
is the sign bit (1 = negative, 0 = non-negative). Negation is achieved by inverting all bits
and adding 1.

Example (8-bit two's complement):
@example
  0 = 0x00 = 00000000
  1 = 0x01 = 00000001
-1 = 0xFF = 11111111 (invert 00000000, add 1)
127 = 0x7F = 01111111
-128 = 0x80 = 10000000
@end example

@subsection Cross-Language Notes

@itemize
@item COBOL: @code{PIC S9(n)} declarations with USAGE BINARY or COMP
@item FORTRAN: @code{INTEGER} (typically signed by default)
@item Forth: Arithmetic operators on signed integers
@item Objective-C: @code{int}, @code{long}, @code{short}
@item Pascal: @code{integer}, @code{shortint}, @code{longint}
@item C-based languages: Default @code{int} type
@end itemize

@node Decimal (BCD)
@section Decimal (BCD)

@subsection Characteristics

DECIMAL types use Binary-Coded Decimal representation, where each decimal digit (0-9) is stored
in 4 bits (a nibble). This provides decimal semantics with fixed-point arithmetic.

@table @asis
@item Size
N digits × 4 bits per digit + 4 bits for sign (or packed format)

@item Range
For N digits: 0 to 10^N - 1

@item Signedness
Can be signed or unsigned depending on SIGN specification

@item Decimal Places
Specified separately via PICTURE clause (e.g., @code{PIC 9(5)V99} = 5 digits before decimal, 2 after)

@item Overflow Behavior
Depends on implementation; typically truncates or errors

@item Example: 4-digit BCD unsigned
@code{PIC 9(4)} = 4 decimal digits, range 0-9999, stored in 2 bytes (8 nibbles, 4 used)

@item Example: 6-digit BCD signed
@code{PIC S9(6)} = 6 decimal digits with sign, range -999999 to 999999, stored in 4 bytes
@end table

@subsection Decimal Representation Detail

In packed BCD (most common):
@itemize
@item Two digits per byte: high nibble (4 bits) + low nibble (4 bits)
@item Sign occupies the low nibble of the last byte: C=positive, D=negative (EBCDIC)
@item Decimal point is implicit (specified in PICTURE clause, not stored)
@end itemize

Example (6-digit BCD with PICTURE 9(4)V99):
@example
Value: 1234.56
Stored: 12 34 56 C  (four bytes: digits 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, and sign C)
@end example

@subsection Arithmetic with DECIMAL (BCD)

When performing arithmetic with DECIMAL operands:

@itemize
@item Addition: Decimal digit-by-digit addition, carry propagates, decimal point preserved
@item Subtraction: Decimal digit-by-digit subtraction, borrow propagates, sign adjusts
@item Multiplication: Result scale is sum of operand scales (requires GIVING clause)
@item Division: Result scale determined by explicit GIVING clause specification
@item Rounding: Occurs at precision boundary specified in PICTURE clause
@end itemize

@subsection Precision and Scale

DECIMAL types have explicit precision (total digits) and scale (digits after decimal point):

@table @asis
@item PICTURE 9(5)
Precision=5, scale=0 (5 integer digits, no fractional part)

@item PICTURE 9(3)V99
Precision=5, scale=2 (3 integer digits, 2 fractional digits)

@item PICTURE S9(7)V999
Precision=10, scale=3 (7 integer digits, 3 fractional digits, signed)
@end table

@subsection Cross-Language Notes

@itemize
@item COBOL: @code{PIC 9(n)[Vfrac]} with USAGE PACKED-DECIMAL or COMP-3
@item FORTRAN: @code{REAL} types approximate; true BCD is not standard
@item Most other languages lack native BCD support; COBOL is specialized here
@end itemize

@node Display Numeric
@section Display Numeric

@subsection Characteristics

DISPLAY numeric types store numbers as character strings in ASCII or EBCDIC encoding, with
optional embedded decimal point or sign characters.

@table @asis
@item Size
N + 1 bytes (N for digits, 1 for sign character or embedded)

@item Range
Limited by string length; typically 0-9...9 or -9...9

@item Signedness
Specified via SIGN IS SEPARATE or SIGN IS LEADING/TRAILING

@item Overflow Behavior
Truncates or errors (depends on implementation)

@item Example: DISPLAY with PIC 9(4)
@code{PIC 9(4)} = "1234" (4 bytes, ASCII digits)

@item Example: DISPLAY signed with PIC S9(4)
@code{PIC S9(4)} = "+1234" or "-1234" (5 bytes, digit + ASCII digits)
@end table

@subsection Representation Detail

DISPLAY numbers are stored as zoned decimal (EBCDIC) or standard ASCII:

EBCDIC zoned decimal:
@itemize
@item Positive: High nibble = 0xF (represented as '0'-'9' in EBCDIC)
@item Negative: High nibble = 0xD (represented as 'A'-'I' in EBCDIC)
@end itemize

Example (COBOL DISPLAY with PIC S9(4)):
@example
Value: +1234
Stored (EBCDIC): F1 F2 F3 F4  (zone bits all 0xF, digits 1,2,3,4)

Value: -1234
Stored (EBCDIC): F1 F2 F3 D4  (zone bits 0xF except last, which is 0xD, digit 4 modified)
@end example

@subsection Arithmetic with DISPLAY

Direct arithmetic on DISPLAY numbers is not typically supported. They must be converted
to BINARY or DECIMAL types first:

@itemize
@item Move to BINARY variable, perform arithmetic, move back to DISPLAY
@item Move to DECIMAL variable, perform arithmetic, move back to DISPLAY
@item Explicit conversion functions in target language
@end itemize

@subsection Cross-Language Notes

@itemize
@item COBOL: @code{PIC 9(n)} or @code{PIC X(n)} with numeric content (USAGE DISPLAY)
@item BASIC: String representation of numbers (then converted for arithmetic)
@item Most languages: Strings + explicit conversion functions
@end itemize

@node Type Conversion and Coercion
@section Type Conversion and Coercion

@subsection Implicit Conversions

When operands of different types are mixed in an arithmetic operation, conversions occur:

@table @asis
@item DISPLAY → BINARY
Parse the string, convert digits to numeric value

@item DISPLAY → DECIMAL
Parse the string including decimal point position, convert to BCD

@item BINARY → DECIMAL
Direct value transfer with appropriate scale application

@item BINARY → DISPLAY
Convert numeric value to ASCII/EBCDIC string representation

@item DECIMAL → BINARY
Truncate fractional part, transfer integer portion

@item DECIMAL → DISPLAY
Format with zone bits or ASCII characters as required
@end table

@subsection Explicit Conversions (Language-Specific)

Each language provides explicit conversion mechanisms:

@itemize
@item COBOL: MOVE source TO destination (implicit); no explicit cast syntax
@item FORTRAN: INT(), REAL(), type-specific conversion functions
@item Objective-C: Type casts with @code{(type) value}
@item Pascal: @code{integer(x)}, @code{real(x)} functions
@item Lua: @code{tonumber()}, @code{tostring()}, @code{math.floor()}
@end itemize

@subsection Conversion Precision Loss

Conversions can lose precision or range:

@itemize
@item BINARY (32-bit) → DECIMAL (8 digits): Max 99,999,999 < 4,294,967,295 (loss of range)
@item DECIMAL (5,2) → BINARY: Fractional part lost if BINARY is integer-only
@item DISPLAY "1.234" → BINARY: Decimal point must be in correct position or conversion fails
@end itemize

@node Overflow and Underflow Behavior
@section Overflow and Underflow Behavior

@subsection BINARY Type Overflow

@table @asis
@item Behavior
Silent wrap-around (modulo 2^N)

@item Example: 8-bit BINARY
255 + 1 = 0 (wraps)
0 - 1 = 255 (wraps)

@item Detection
Requires explicit comparison to check range or use platform-specific flags (carry/borrow)

@item Language Support
Most languages don't detect automatically; platform/compiler-specific exceptions possible
@end table

@subsection BINARY SIGNED Type Overflow

@table @asis
@item Behavior
Silent wrap-around with sign changes (two's complement)

@item Example: 8-bit BINARY SIGNED
127 + 1 = -128 (wraps)
-128 - 1 = 127 (wraps)

@item Detection
Requires comparison or platform-specific flag checking

@item Language Support
Some C compilers warn with @code{-Woverflow}; FORTRAN has CHECK= options
@end table

@subsection DECIMAL Type Overflow

@table @asis
@item Behavior
Depends on implementation: truncation (most common), error, or rounding to max value

@item Example: PIC 9(4) with value 10000
Typically: 10000 truncated to 9999 or error raised

@item Fractional Overflow
Example: PIC 9(3)V99 = 999.99 max
Adding 0.01 → 1000.00 (10 digits) → requires GIVING clause to specify result size

@item Detection
Platform-dependent; COBOL may set status code; some languages don't detect at all

@item Language Support
COBOL: May set status variable; FORTRAN: compile-time options; most others: no automatic detection
@end table

@subsection Underflow (DECIMAL Fractional)

@table @asis
@item Behavior
Fractional part lost or rounded, depending on rounding mode

@item Example: PIC 9(3)V2 (scale=2) with intermediate result 123.456
Stored as 123.46 (rounded) or 123.45 (truncated)

@item Detection
Depends on rounding specification in declaration or operation
@end table

@node Rounding Modes
@section Rounding Modes

DECIMAL arithmetic specifies rounding at scale boundaries. Four standard modes:

@table @asis
@item TRUNCATE (Round toward zero)
Fractional part discarded regardless of value

@item HALF-UP (Round half to positive infinity)
Standard rounding: 0.5 rounds up, 0.4 rounds down

@item HALF-EVEN (Round half to even, banker's rounding)
Fractional part 0.5 rounds to nearest even digit

@item TOWARD-INF (Round toward positive infinity)
Always rounds up (ceiling function)
@end table

@subsection Language Support

@table @asis
@item COBOL
Controlled by compiler directive or explicit ROUNDING clause in DIVIDE statement

@item FORTRAN
NINT (nearest integer), INT (truncate), CEILING, FLOOR functions available

@item Others
Platform/compiler-dependent; manual rounding via multiplication/division common
@end table

@subsection Examples

8-digit DECIMAL number with PIC 9(5)V3 (scale=3), rounding modes applied to 1234.567:

@table @asis
@item Original Value
1234.567 (7 significant digits, 3 fractional)

@item TRUNCATE
1234.567 → 1234 (fractional discarded)

@item HALF-UP
1234.567 → 1235 (0.567 > 0.5, rounds up)

@item HALF-EVEN
1234.567 → 1235 (0.5 rounds to even: 1234=even, 1235=odd → rounds to 1235)
Actually: 1234.6 would round to 1234 (even), 1234.5 also rounds to 1234 (even)

@item TOWARD-INF
1234.567 → 1235 (ceiling)
@end table

@node Precision and Scale
@section Precision and Scale

@subsection Definitions

@table @asis
@item Precision
Total number of significant digits (before and after decimal point)

@item Scale
Number of digits after the decimal point (fractional digits)

@item Integer Digits
Precision - Scale

@end table

@subsection PICTURE Clause Notation (COBOL example)

@table @asis
@item PIC 9(5)
Precision=5, Scale=0 (5 digits, no decimal point)

@item PIC 9(5)V99
Precision=7, Scale=2 (5 digits before V, 2 after = 7 total; implicit decimal point)

@item PIC S9(6)V999
Precision=9, Scale=3 (signed, 6 integer digits, 3 fractional digits)

@item PIC 9(3).99
Precision=5, Scale=2 (3 digits, then decimal point, 2 fractional)

@end table

@subsection Arithmetic Result Precision

When arithmetic operations combine operands of different scales:

@itemize
@item Addition: Result scale = MAX(operand scales); precision = max precision needed
@item Subtraction: Result scale = MAX(operand scales); precision = max precision needed
@item Multiplication: Result scale = sum of operand scales; precision = sum of precisions (can double)
@item Division: Result scale specified explicitly; precision determined by operation requirements
@end itemize

@subsection Precision Loss Scenarios

@enumerate
@item Division by number larger than dividend: Result < 1.0; decimal result stored exactly if scale sufficient
@item Multiplication of two large numbers: Result scale is sum of operand scales; may exceed storage capacity
@item Rounding to smaller scale: Fractional part lost; stored as integer multiple of 10^(-scale)
@end enumerate

@end node
